----------------------------------------------------------------------
Artistic references (inspiration only — no code copied)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The "fluid-volume" and "endless-shallows" presets are original
reimplementations whose look was inspired by publicly shared CodePen studies
(Sabo Sugi). No source code from those references is used or redistributed;
the shaders are written from scratch using Sonara's own shader prelude.

The cosine-palette formula used by "iridescent-bloom" follows the freely
published technique by Inigo Quilez.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
ThreeUI / Design+Code (github.com/MengTo/threeui)
License: MIT
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The "predictive-arc" visual preset (shared/canvas-scene-runtime.mjs) ports the
`dot-grid-arc-field` technique from ThreeUI's "Predictive Arc" renderer
(src/shaders/predictive-arc/predictiveArcRenderer.ts). The implementation was
rewritten for Sonara's Canvas 2D compositing path and reuses Sonara's own
color/common controls; it does not require or bundle the @designcodeio/threeui
package. The upstream MIT license text appears above.

The "data-pixel-arc" visual preset ports the Data Pixel variant
(src/shaders/data-pixel-arc/dataPixelArcRenderer.ts) and the "ribbon-field"
visual preset ports the Ribbon Field variant
(src/shaders/ribbon-field/ribbonFieldShaders.ts) of the same collection. Both
were rewritten for Sonara's runtime (Canvas 2D and WebGL 1 / GLSL ES 1.00
respectively) and reuse Sonara's own color/common controls.

Five further variants of the same technique family were adapted from the
"neuform-isolated" sources (MIT), completing the family at eight variants:

  "signal-particles"   src/shaders/neuform-isolated/sources/signal-particles.html
  "override-grid"      src/shaders/neuform-isolated/sources/override-grid.html
  "void-field"         src/shaders/neuform-isolated/sources/void-protocol.html
  "halftone-flow"      src/shaders/neuform-isolated/sources/nexus-unified-flow.html
  "amber-halftone"     src/shaders/neuform-isolated/sources/amber-halftone.html

The techniques (cross-wave point activation with rare hashed highlights;
radial-pulse block grid; barrel-distorted breathing dot matrix with scanlines;
domain-warped flow painted as a halftone; sinusoidal radial point grid with a
vertical phosphor gradient) were reimplemented for Sonara's shared runtime so
preview and export use the same code path. Upstream's per-frame time
accumulators were replaced by the runtime's deterministic time, and upstream's
fixed palettes were replaced by Sonara preset colors. Three deliberate
departures: the mouse-parallax uniform in void-protocol was removed (the Sonara
runtime is deterministic and pointer-free); the float loop counter in
nexus-unified-flow was converted to an int (GLSL ES 1.00 has no float loop
index); and the three.js point cloud in amber-halftone became a procedural
fragment-shader grid. None of these presets require or bundle the
@designcodeio/threeui package.

The "laser" visual preset ports four variants of ThreeUI's Laser shader
(src/shaders/laser/laserShaders.ts, MIT) as four variations of a single Sonara
preset, with the variant selected by a uniform:

  blade  atmospheric-blade  — fog-borne beam with mirage and contact point
  array  vanishing-array    — angular spokes decaying with radius, plus rings
  prism  prism-aperture     — diamond aperture with red/blue dispersion fringes
  relay  halftone-relay     — twin beams, horizontal relay and a halftone grid

The techniques (Gaussian beam profile, fbm fog, angular spokes, dispersion
fringes, halftone relay) were reimplemented for Sonara's WebGL 1 / GLSL ES 1.00
fullscreen u_* interface, reusing Sonara's own color and common controls. Five
deliberate departures:

  1. The pointer uniform was REPLACED BY USER CONTROLS. Upstream lerps the
     pointer per frame from the mouse position, which is not reproducible; export
     determinism is a hard Sonara rule. The three Sonara controls offsetX,
     offsetY and rotation move and turn the central element deterministically,
     with a slow u_time-only drift kept underneath for motion.
  2. The u_hue and u_saturation uniforms became Sonara preset colors.
  3. The upstream `length` uniform was renamed `span` — `length` is a reserved
     GLSL builtin and fails to compile.
  4. Upstream's HSV accent helper was replaced by mixes of the preset palette.
  5. The four variants are Sonara preset *variations*, not four separate
     presets, so they share one entry in the atmosphere browser and use the
     existing variation picker.

The upstream Reinhard tonemap (color / (color + 0.72)) was kept: it is what
keeps the background black and the beam core reading as light rather than paint.

The "crt" visual preset ports the CRT *technique* from ThreeUI's CRT shader
(src/shaders/crt/crtShaders.ts, MIT): tube curvature, scanline roll, phosphor
triad mask, halation, rolling bar, glass sheen, vignette, flicker and grain. The
four variations are audio-scope contents, all generated inside the fragment
shader:

  oscillo   scope trace   — phosphor waveform with core and tail
  spectrum  spectrum bars — bars with smoothed peak caps
  vector    vector scope  — grid with a rotating sweep and trail
  tunnel    depth rings   — receding rings toward a bright core

The tube techniques were reimplemented for Sonara's WebGL 1 / GLSL ES 1.00
fullscreen u_* interface. Six deliberate departures, the first three of which
exist for legal reasons rather than technical ones:

  1. THE SCREEN CONTENT IS NOT PORTED. Upstream samples a texture painted with
     third-party screens, and Sonara ships none of them. Excluded by name:

       - the "blue-screen" style, which reproduces the Microsoft Windows blue
         screen: the #161d92 ground, the wording of its fault text, its
         STOP: 0x… and RASTER.SYS module lines, and its "press any key to
         restart" prompt;
       - the "nintendo" style, whose screen evokes a registered console
         trademark;
       - the "terminal" style, whose text is The Matrix's ZION mainframe,
         the Nebuchadnezzar, "Hacking Matrix grid nodes" and /dev/mind.

     Sonara's CRT therefore generates its own content in the shader, with no
     letter, no logo and no mark of any kind. The exclusion is structural: with
     no screen texture, there is no place a third-party screen could reappear.
  2. "cinematic" was not carried over as a style; the glass character it
     described is expressed through Sonara's own controls.
  3. The ~16 upstream style uniforms were reduced to six controls plus the
     variation index, because Sonara exposes seven u_param slots. Tube speed is
     the existing common `speed` control rather than a duplicate slider.
  4. The u_uTex sampler and its offscreen canvas were dropped: the content is
     procedural, so one fullscreen pass replaces the two-pass pipeline.
  5. u_hue and u_saturation became Sonara preset colors.
  6. The four variations are Sonara preset *variations*, not four separate
     presets, sharing one browser entry and the existing variation picker.

The scanline density is derived from the render height rather than fixed in
lines, so the control keeps its effect and the appearance matches between
preview and export at any resolution.

A note on method: an earlier draft of this work classified candidate effects by
host and tier (Canvas 2D, WebGL, three.js) and by effort. That classification
rated the CRT "useful, exclude the Nintendo variant" and the real problems —
a Microsoft screen and a Warner Bros. screen, neither of which has anything to
do with the host — stayed invisible. Auditing by content (what a shader draws)
rather than by host is what surfaced them.

